# sow_witness.rish -- prove the projected seed is clean before it is ever public.
#
# The heart of the two-grain breach: an additive privacy boundary cannot leak
# what it never received, and this witness proves the seed received nothing
# personal. A fix is closed by a witness on metal, never a claim
# (.claude/rules/reds-first.md).
#
# Duties:
#   0. The wall can red -- the identity scan is shown a planted name and answers.
#   1. M1 -- the manifest classifies every tracked root path, exactly once.
#   2. Project the seed fresh from the field.
#   3. IDENT -- no maintainer identity string survives into seed/.
#   4. NO_PERSONAL -- no `personal` path and no key material appears in seed/.
#
# WHY DUTY 0 STANDS AHEAD OF THE OTHERS. Duties 1 through 4 all end in the same shape: a scan
# runs over the projected seed and this witness asserts its clean word is present. That proves
# the seed is clean only if the scan could have said otherwise, and until 20260908 nothing in
# this tree had ever planted a maintainer name in front of tools/fixtures/s/sow_leak_scan.sh
# and watched it answer. The grain's own strand is the reason it matters here more than
# anywhere: a guard that cannot red guards nothing, so a witness must be proven able to make
# a sound before its silence means anything (REDS row 59).
#
# This wall in particular has already been proven able to red FALSELY and never to red truly.
# REDS %244: a blanket history rewrite turned two of the scan's own entries into the public
# pseudonym, and 22 projected files failed a privacy check that had nothing to do with privacy.
# A guard whose only demonstrated red is a wrong one is the guard whose green is worth least.
#
# The control costs no projection -- it builds isolated pen directories and reads each with the
# scan's own working-directory-relative `seed`, so the real seed/ is never touched and custody
# gate %1 is nowhere near it. It reports two further readings it deliberately does NOT gate:
# a name inside a binary file is invisible to the scan's -I, and an ABSENT seed/ reads clean.
# The second is covered here by ORDERING rather than by the scan -- duty 2 below projects fresh
# and asserts SOW_OK before duty 3 reads anything -- which is why the duties run in this order
# and why moving duty 3 ahead of duty 2 would turn this witness green over nothing at all.
#
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/s/sow_witness.rish

# Duty 0 -- the wall can red
let ctl = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/s/sow_leak_control.sh"]
say ctl.out
assert ctl.ok else "the identity-wall control failed to run"
assert (ctl.out contains "control_verdict=ok") else "the identity scan did not red on a planted maintainer name"

# Duty 1 -- manifest completeness (M1)
let cover = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/s/sow_manifest_cover.sh"]
say cover.out
assert cover.ok else "manifest-cover scan failed to run"
assert (cover.out contains "M1_OK") else "manifest does not classify every root path exactly once"

# Duty 2 -- project fresh
let proj = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/s/sow_project.sh"]
say proj.out
assert proj.ok else "sow projection failed to run"
assert (proj.out contains "SOW_OK") else "sow projection did not complete"

# Duty 3 -- the negative that matters most
let leak = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/s/sow_leak_scan.sh"]
say leak.out
assert leak.ok else "identity leak scan failed to run"
assert (leak.out contains "IDENT_CLEAN") else "a maintainer identity string leaked into the seed"

# Duty 4 -- no personal path, no key material
let pers = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/s/sow_personal_scan.sh"]
say pers.out
assert pers.ok else "personal-path scan failed to run"
assert (pers.out contains "NO_PERSONAL") else "a personal path or key appeared in the seed"

say "SOW_WITNESS_GREEN seed/ is projected and provably clean"
